Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP)
Haematology and Oncology
Diffuse dependent petechiae/purpura, Spontaneous mucosal bleeding, Absence of splenomegaly/lymphadenopathy, Isolated profound thrombocytopenia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-026
Reason for Visit: Spontaneous mucosal bleeding and rapid petechial rash development requiring immediate assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient exhibiting spontaneous, unremitting epistaxis combined with a rapidly spreading non-blanching petechial rash. Urgent in-person assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate for profound, life-threatening thrombocytopenia and establish haemodynamic stability. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed a catastrophic risk of fatal internal haemorrhage, explicitly satisfying criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Their nose started pouring blood an hour ago for no reason, and they have these dark purple bruises all over their legs even though they have not bumped into anything.
History of Present Illness: The caregiver reports a 24-hour linear evolution of cutaneous symptoms, beginning with widespread, unprovoked superficial ecchymoses on the shins. Over the past 12 hours, this progressed to the sudden appearance of diffuse petechiae primarily located on the dependent lower extremities. One hour ago, the patient developed spontaneous severe epistaxis that remains difficult to control. They experienced a mild, self-limiting viral upper respiratory infection two weeks prior. General health is otherwise intact, with a complete absence of high fevers, bone pain, or weight loss.
Functional Status: Independent but resting quietly due to active bleeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver is highly anxious regarding the visible blood loss and sudden rash.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 32 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused patient applying continuous direct pressure to the anterior nares.
Physical Exam:
Skin: Widespread, diffuse pinpoint petechiae predominantly on the bilateral lower extremities below the knees. Multiple large, painless ecchymoses noted on the shins and forearms without localized swelling.
HEENT: Active, slow venous oozing from the right anterior nasal septum. Normal conjunctivae.
Abdomen: Soft, completely non-tender. Complete absence of palpable splenomegaly or hepatomegaly.
Lymphatic: Complete absence of prominent cervical, axillary, or inguinal lymphadenopathy.
Neurological: Grossly intact. No focal deficits.
Environmental Observations: Multiple blood-soaked tissues in the immediate vicinity.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent NSAID or aspirin administration.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of palpable splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, or lymphadenopathy, alongside a lack of generalized bone pain.

Meningococcal Septicaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the patient appearing well and non-toxic, with a normal temperature and stable haemodynamics.

Non-Accidental Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the presence of a true petechial rash and spontaneous mucosal bleeding, indicating a systemic haematological defect rather than localized blunt force trauma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of spontaneous mucosal bleeding and diffuse petechiae in an otherwise well patient strongly indicates acute severe thrombocytopenia. The physical examination explicitly rules out hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy, severely lowering the likelihood of an infiltrative malignant process like leukaemia, while pointing directly toward an immune-mediated platelet destruction mechanism. However, the presence of active epistaxis signifies an unacceptably high risk for spontaneous internal bleeding, including catastrophic intracranial haemorrhage. This acute, life-threatening haemorrhagic potential unequivocally required an urgent after-hours assessment to secure haemostasis and facilitate immediate hospital transfer, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of topical adrenaline-soaked gauze to the anterior nares achieved partial haemostasis. Immediate referral and ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent full blood count, anticipating isolated profound thrombocytopenia below 30 x 10^9/L.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Maintain constant, firm pressure on the soft part of the nose without letting go to check. By keeping the pressure uninterrupted for ten full minutes, you allow the fragile initial clot to form a solid plug over the bleeding vessel.

Keep the head leaning slightly forward while sitting upright. By angling the airway down, you prevent blood from draining into the stomach, which actively stops the nausea and vomiting reflex.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is mandated. If the patient develops a sudden severe headache, exhibits confusion, or begins vomiting blood prior to paramedic arrival, inform the dispatch operator immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical findings indicate a severe deficiency in blood-clotting cells; definitive laboratory blood tests at the hospital are required to confirm this diagnosis and rule out other bone marrow conditions.
Clinical Course: The immune system is temporarily attacking the platelets. The hospital will monitor the blood counts closely and may provide specific intravenous medications to stop the bleeding and boost the cell numbers.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not administer any ibuprofen or related pain relievers. Wait for the ambulance to arrive. Keep the patient seated quietly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The blood is missing the tiny cells that form scabs, which is why the nosebleed will not stop normally and bruises are appearing spontaneously. The hospital needs to run urgent blood tests tonight to confirm the numbers and provide specialized treatments to protect against severe internal bleeding.
Actionable Steps: Sit still. Keep applying pressure to the nose. Do not blow the nose under any circumstances.

